<article class="env-lemma" id="0H3W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H3W">Lemma <span data-tag="0H3W">33.25.11</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme of finite type over a field $k$. There exists a finite purely inseparable extension $k'/k$, an integer $t \geq 0$, and closed subschemes </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X_{k'} \supset Z_0 \supset Z_1 \supset \ldots \supset Z_ t = \emptyset  \]
</div>
  <p> such that $Z_0 = (X_{k'})_{red}$ and $Z_ i \setminus Z_{i + 1}$ is smooth over $k'$ for all $i$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may use induction on $\dim (X)$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/04KT" data-tag="04KT">33.6.11</a> we can find a finite purely inseparable extension $k'/k$ such that $(X_{k'})_{red}$ is geometrically reduced over $k'$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/056V" data-tag="056V">33.25.7</a> there is a nowhere dense closed subscheme $X' \subset (X_{k'})_{red}$ such that $(X_{k'})_{red} \setminus X'$ is smooth over $k'$. Then $\dim (X') < \dim (X)$. By induction hypothesis there exists a finite purely inseparable extension $k''/k'$, an integer $t' \geq 0$, and closed subschemes 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  X'_{k''} \supset Y_0 \supset Y_1 \supset \ldots \supset Y_{t'} = \emptyset  \]
</div>
    <p>
       such that $Y_0 = (X'_{k''})_{red}$ and $Y_ i \setminus Y_{i + 1}$ is smooth over $k''$ for all $i$. Then we let $t = t' + 1$ and we consider 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  X_{k''} \supset Z_0 \supset Z_1 \supset \ldots \supset Z_ t = \emptyset  \]
</div>
    <p>
       given by $Z_0 = (X_{k''})_{red}$ and $Z_ i = Y_{i - 1}$ for $i > 0$; this makes sense as $X'_{k''}$ is a closed subscheme of $X_{k''}$. We omit the verification that all the stated properties hold. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>